WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.400
If a bird lays its eggs earlier every spring, is it keeping up?

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.220
The first film measured a gap in space, between a species and its isotherm.

3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.660
This film measures a similar gap in time.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:21.343
The seasonal timing of life events is called phenology.

5
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:26.463
Leafing, flowering, migration and egg laying are all phenology.

6
00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:30.823
As springs have warmed, many of these now come earlier.

7
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:34.073
Now one bird and its food.

8
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:38.493
The gold curve is the caterpillar peak, the food supply.

9
00:00:39.753 --> 00:00:42.433
The green bar is when nestlings need feeding most.

10
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:44.853
Here they start in step.

11
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:49.983
Warm the springs. The gold peak slides earlier.

12
00:00:51.143 --> 00:00:53.823
The green bar slides earlier too, but by less.

13
00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:56.983
A red bracket can open between them.

14
00:00:57.983 --> 00:00:59.803
That gap is trophic mismatch.

15
00:01:01.423 --> 00:01:03.623
Demand now peaks after the food peak has passed.

16
00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:08.003
So an earlier date, on its own, cannot show keeping up.

17
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.647
Does mismatch cost birds?

18
00:01:12.327 --> 00:01:16.367
Christiaan Both and colleagues compared nine Dutch populations of the pied

19
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:21.447
flycatcher. It winters in Africa and breeds in European woods.

20
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:25.547
In some of the forests, caterpillars peaked early.

21
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:30.967
In others they peaked late, and birds that bred early were still well timed.

22
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:37.100
Where food peaked early, flycatcher numbers fell by about ninety percent over two

23
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.340
decades. Where it peaked late, declines were weak at most.

24
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.840
The early forests lost about nine pairs in every ten.

25
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.920
Does that comparison prove that mismatch caused the loss?

26
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:01.567
Not yet. It supports a cost of mismatch, but it compares sites.

27
00:02:02.927 --> 00:02:07.047
The forests were not assigned their caterpillar timing, so something else could

28
00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:08.047
differ between them.

29
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.160
A second question.

30
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.820
What tells a bird when to lay?

31
00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.660
In the field, warm springs bring early laying, but they also bring early food.

32
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.020
The pattern alone cannot separate them.

33
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Marcel Visser and colleagues kept breeding pairs of great tits in climatized

34
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.180
aviaries. Every aviary had food freely available.

35
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.440
Only the temperature pattern differed, warm or cold.

36
00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:40.173
The warm aviaries generally laid earlier.

37
00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:46.653
With food unlimited, the authors read temperature as likely acting as a cue, a

38
00:02:46.653 --> 00:02:48.353
signal the birds respond to.

39
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:51.647
Each design answers its own question.

40
00:02:52.907 --> 00:02:54.467
A trend suggests a change.

41
00:02:55.807 --> 00:02:59.067
Testing it against chance and natural variation is detection.

42
00:03:00.347 --> 00:03:05.307
The forest comparison links food timing to decline, but sites can differ in other

43
00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:10.407
ways. The aviaries show temperature acting on laying, in great tits.

44
00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:14.787
Through all of it, the date alone never answered the question.

45
00:03:16.087 --> 00:03:18.887
What counted was the gap between the food and the family.
